<!DOCTYPE node PUBLIC "-//freedesktop//DTD D-BUS Object Introspection 1.0//EN"
 "http://www.freedesktop.org/standards/dbus/1.0/introspect.dtd">
<!--
  org.ruyaos.Session1 — launch installed apps as a profile, stop them, list what runs.
  Served by ruya-session on the system bus at /org/ruyaos/Session1 (core T-139, in the image T-162;
  the shell's mock backend follows this contract too). It runs as root; an app is a transient unit
  of the profile user's own systemd manager, and `instance` is that unit's name.
  Contract between shell (Qt/QML) and services (Rust); see docs/specs/03-services-and-apps.md §4.1.

  Trust: the `profile` argument of Launch is trusted. The bus policy lets only ruya-shell and root
  call this interface, and the shell passes the active profile. The service nevertheless refuses
  to run anything as a non-profile account (root, system users, ruya-shell): such a name is
  "unknown-profile". Apps run as the profile's Unix user, never as ruya-shell or root (ADR-004).

  Display (T-164, ADR-023; experimental for apps): every app unit gets
  WAYLAND_DISPLAY=/run/ruya-wayland/ruya-<id> (the profile's own socket, made by the compositor,
  0600 + ACL for that user only) and SDL_VIDEODRIVER=wayland. The interface itself is unchanged.

  Status strings are stable API:
    "ok"                success (for Launch: the app is starting, AppStarted follows when it runs)
    "unknown-app"       the app id is not installed
    "unknown-profile"   no such profile, or not an account apps may run as
    "already-running"   this app is already running (or starting) for this profile
    "unknown-instance"  no running instance with that name
    "invalid"           malformed argument (empty id, bad characters)
    "failed"            internal error

  Freeze and Thaw (T-457, ADR-033 point 3) have these extra statuses:
    "not-running"       no instance with that name is running (unknown, still starting or stopping)
    "not-allowed"       the app is not the signed-in profile's (or nobody is signed in)

  A profile that leaves has its apps frozen (T-165, ADR-004): the service follows Profiles1's
  ActiveProfile (PropertiesChanged, and a restart of Profiles1). When it changes from a profile P
  to anything else (another profile, or "" when the console locks: a switch from the Ruya Hub or
  the picker, a sign-out, the idle lock), every running app of P (its unit and every scope, as
  Freeze) is frozen, and FrozenChanged(instance, true) follows each. An app frozen already is left
  as it is; an app still starting or stopping is not frozen. The first value the service reads
  after its own start freezes the running apps of every profile but the signed-in one. The value
  is read in the freezer's turn (after the Freeze and Thaw calls that came before), so a Thaw sent
  just before the change still ends frozen. Nothing is thawed by a sign-in: the shell sends Thaw
  for the signed-in profile's frozen apps (Thaw refuses another profile's app, "not-allowed"); Stop
  works on any frozen app. There is no extra method: the frozen state is the running record's.

  InstanceOfPid (T-459) has this extra status:
    "not-found"         the process is in no running instance (no such process, or not an app's)

  Usage ledger (T-247; ReportPresence, ListSessions, GetTotals, ClearHistory, UsageChanged) has
  these extra statuses:
    "not-allowed"       the profile is not the active one (reads), or the actor may not clear it
    "wrong-pin"         ClearHistory: the admin PIN did not match (attempts_left says how many remain)
    "locked-out"        ClearHistory: too many failures (retry_after_secs says when to try again)

  An instance is an opaque string chosen by the service (a systemd unit name); the shell only
  hands it back. Every list is a plain array and every record an a{sv} dictionary, so fields can be
  added later without breaking clients (unknown keys are ignored).

  Running record (a{sv}), keys:
    instance   s    opaque instance name
    app_id     s    Flatpak application id
    profile    s    profile id the app runs as
    started    x    unix seconds
    state      s    "starting" | "running" | "stopping"
    frozen     b    true while the app is frozen by Freeze (T-457) or because its profile left (T-165);
               FrozenChanged follows each change
               (an app taken over after a restart of the service reads its unit's FreezerState)

  Usage ledger (T-247). ruya-session keeps, per profile, a ledger of play sessions under
  /var/lib/ruya-session/usage/<profile>/ (root only, 0600). A session is one run of one app; only
  times and the exit reason are recorded, never anything about the content. Rules (spec 03):
    - time counts only while the profile is active, not locked or suspended, the app is the
      foreground app and the shell reports "active"; durations come from the monotonic clock;
    - the shell reports who is in front with ReportPresence on every change and every 20 s; a
      report older than 75 s counts as "unknown" and counts nothing;
    - the service saves a heartbeat every 30 s; a session found at start-up (crash, power cut)
      ends at the last heartbeat; sessions under 10 s are dropped;
    - a profile switch ends the previous profile's sessions;
    - raw sessions are kept 90 days, then rolled up into daily totals kept 24 months;
    - the shell reads the active profile only: ListSessions and GetTotals answer "not-allowed"
      for any other profile (the history of another profile is not browsable, admin or not).

  Session record (a{sv}), keys:
    app_id              s    Flatpak application id
    app_version         s    version at the start ("" if unknown); an update mid-session continues
                             the session
    start               x    unix seconds (UTC)
    end                 x    unix seconds (UTC)
    foreground_seconds  u    seconds the app was the foreground, not idle or suspended
    exit_reason         s    "quit" | "crashed" | "killed" | "time-limit" | "bedtime"
    open                b    true while the app still runs (end is then "now")
    day                 s    "YYYY-MM-DD": the first local day (device time zone) with counted time in the session

  Day row (a{sv}), keys (one row per app and local day; the day is in the device time zone):
    app_id     s    Flatpak application id
    day        s    "YYYY-MM-DD"
    seconds    u    foreground seconds on that day
    sessions   u    sessions whose first day with counted time is that day
    longest    u    seconds of the longest of those sessions
    first      x    unix seconds: the earliest start among the sessions touching that day
    last       x    unix seconds: the latest end among them
-->
<node>
  <interface name="org.ruyaos.Session1">

    <!-- Starts the app as the profile. The answer comes as soon as it is queued; the app is
         running when AppStarted arrives. If it never gets there, AppExited with reason
         "launch-failed" is sent instead. `instance` is "" unless the status is "ok". -->
    <method name="Launch">
      <arg name="app_id" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="profile" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="status" type="s" direction="out"/>
      <arg name="instance" type="s" direction="out"/>
    </method>

    <!-- Asks the app to quit, and kills it if it does not. AppExited (reason "quit", or "killed"
         after the kill) follows. Stopping an instance that is already stopping is "ok". -->
    <method name="Stop">
      <arg name="instance" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="status" type="s" direction="out"/>
    </method>

    <!-- Freezes a running app of the signed-in profile (T-457, the Ruya Hub pauses the game): the
         cgroup freezer (`systemctl --user freeze`) of the app's unit and of every active Flatpak
         scope of the app, named exactly (never a sibling app whose id starts with this one's). Its
         processes stop where they are (no CPU; their sound streams run dry and, T-466, are parked on a
         null sink so the sound card can suspend, unless another app of the profile still runs); the
         last frame stays with the compositor. A frozen app is frozen already: "ok", nothing is done. When the
         freezer fails or does not answer in time, what was frozen is thawed again and the status is
         "failed": the app keeps running. Synchronous: the answer comes when the freeze is done (each
         systemctl is bounded to 5 s). Freeze and Thaw calls are served one at a time, in the order
         they arrive. FrozenChanged(instance, true) follows a change. Statuses: ok,
         invalid, not-running, not-allowed, failed. -->
    <method name="Freeze">
      <arg name="instance" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="status" type="s" direction="out"/>
    </method>

    <!-- Thaws the app again (the same unit and scopes as Freeze); "ok" also when it was not frozen
         (the freezer is asked anyway, so a state the service did not see is repaired). Stop thaws a frozen app before it asks it to quit, so it can handle the SIGTERM.
         FrozenChanged(instance, false) follows a change. Statuses as Freeze. The Thaw puts a parked
         sound stream back where it was (T-466); an app that stayed frozen for 30 s, with swap active,
         had part of its memory moved to swap, so it may fault some of it back in. Neither changes
         this interface. -->
    <method name="Thaw">
      <arg name="instance" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="status" type="s" direction="out"/>
    </method>

    <!-- The running instance a process belongs to (T-459): the compositor asks it for the client
         pid of each new window, because the app id a window gives itself is not always its
         Flatpak id (a pygame game calls itself "python3.12"). The service reads the process's
         cgroup (/proc/<pid>/cgroup) and answers the instance whose unit, or one of whose app's
         Flatpak scopes (`app-flatpak-<app_id>-<n>.scope`, also an Electron app's second one,
         T-435), contains it, in the manager of that instance's profile user. A starting or
         stopping instance counts too. `instance` is "" unless the status is "ok". Statuses: ok,
         invalid (pid 0), not-found. -->
    <method name="InstanceOfPid">
      <arg name="pid" type="u" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="status" type="s" direction="out"/>
      <arg name="instance" type="s" direction="out"/>
    </method>

    <!-- Apps that keep running when ruya-session restarts are still listed (state "running",
         their original start time): the service finds them again before it takes the bus name,
         announces nothing for them (no AppStarted) and reports their end with AppExited. The
         exit code of such an app is the last one the service saw: usually 0, even for an app
         that crashed while the service was not following its process. -->
    <method name="ListRunning">
      <arg name="apps" type="aa{sv}" direction="out"/>
    </method>

    <!-- The shell tells who is in front: `instance` is the foreground app's instance ("" when
         the shell itself is in front), `attention` is "active" | "idle" | "locked" | "suspended".
         `profile` is the active profile ("" when none). Call on every change and every 20 s.
         "invalid" for an unknown attention or a bad profile id. -->
    <method name="ReportPresence">
      <arg name="profile" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="instance" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="attention" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="status" type="s" direction="out"/>
    </method>

    <!-- Sessions of the profile that ended at or after `since` (unix seconds), newest first, at
         most `limit` (capped at 1000). Running apps are included with open = true. Statuses:
         ok, invalid, unknown-profile, not-allowed, failed. -->
    <method name="ListSessions">
      <arg name="profile" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="since" type="x" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="limit" type="u" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="status" type="s" direction="out"/>
      <arg name="sessions" type="aa{sv}" direction="out"/>
    </method>

    <!-- Totals per app and day from `from` to `to` ("YYYY-MM-DD", inclusive; "" = the earliest
         kept / today). `today` is today's date in the device time zone. Rows are sorted by day,
         then app. Same statuses as ListSessions. -->
    <method name="GetTotals">
      <arg name="profile" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="from" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="to" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="status" type="s" direction="out"/>
      <arg name="today" type="s" direction="out"/>
      <arg name="rows" type="aa{sv}" direction="out"/>
    </method>

    <!-- Deletes the profile's whole ledger. With admin_id "" the profile must be the active one
         (its own PIN was checked when it signed in); otherwise admin_id and admin_pin are checked
         with Profiles1.VerifyAdmin. Sessions still running start again from zero. Statuses: ok,
         invalid, unknown-profile, not-allowed, wrong-pin, locked-out, failed. attempts_left and
         retry_after_secs as in Profiles1. T-220's "Clear my play history" is this call. -->
    <method name="ClearHistory">
      <arg name="profile" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="admin_id" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="admin_pin" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="status" type="s" direction="out"/>
      <arg name="attempts_left" type="u" direction="out"/>
      <arg name="retry_after_secs" type="u" direction="out"/>
    </method>

    <!-- The ledger of `profile` changed: a session ended, a heartbeat added time, or it was
         cleared. -->
    <signal name="UsageChanged">
      <arg name="profile" type="s"/>
    </signal>

    <!-- The `frozen` of a running app changed (Freeze, Thaw, or the thaw before a Stop). -->
    <signal name="FrozenChanged">
      <arg name="instance" type="s"/>
      <arg name="frozen" type="b"/>
    </signal>

    <signal name="AppStarted">
      <arg name="instance" type="s"/>
      <arg name="app_id" type="s"/>
    </signal>

    <!-- Sent once per instance. reason: "quit" (stopped on request or ended normally), "crashed"
         (exited abnormally by itself), "killed" (ended by the kill after a Stop it ignored, or by
         a signal from the system while it ran; dying of the Stop's own SIGTERM is "quit"),
         "launch-failed" (never reached running, or its program could not be executed [exit
         status 203/EXEC]; exit_code is then -1). The play-time ledger
         additionally knows "time-limit" and "bedtime" (T-211 ends a session with them). -->
    <signal name="AppExited">
      <arg name="instance" type="s"/>
      <arg name="app_id" type="s"/>
      <arg name="exit_code" type="i"/>
      <arg name="reason" type="s"/>
    </signal>

  </interface>
</node>
